        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.139,
            0.252,
            0.158
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.5 Summary"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.166,
            0.578,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": "In this chapter, you have studied the following points :"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.189,
            0.902,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "**1.** An **arithmetic progression** (AP) is a list of numbers in which each term is obtained by adding a fixed number d to the preceding term, except the first term. The fixed number d is called the **common difference**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.249,
            0.65,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": "The general form of an AP is $a, \\; a+d, \\; a+2d, \\; a+3d, \\; \\ldots$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.273,
            0.898,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": "**2.** A given list of numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}, \\cdots$ is an AP, if the differences $a_{_2} - a_{_1}, a_{_3} - a_{_2},$ $a _ { 4 } - a _ { 3 } , \\ldots , \\mathrm { g i v e }$ the same value, i.e., if $a _ { { } _ { k + 1 } } - a _ { { } _ { k } }$ is the same for different values of k."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.313,
            0.9,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": "**3.** In an AP with first term a and common difference d, the nth term (or the general term) is given by $a_{n}=a+(n-1)$ d."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.358,
            0.585,
            0.376
          ],
          "content": "**4.** The sum of the first n terms of an AP is given by :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.377,
            0.393,
            0.585,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "\\mathbb { S } = \\; { \\frac { n } { 2 } } { \\big [ } 2 a + ( n - 1 ) d { \\big ] }",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.434,
            0.9,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "**5.** If l is the last term of the finite AP, say the nth term, then the sum of all terms of the AP is given by :"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.382,
            0.486,
            0.502,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "S = \\frac{n}{2}(a + l)",
          "image_source": "images/page_23_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.364,
            0.572,
            0.634,
            0.593
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A NOTE TO THE READER"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.62,
            0.855,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": "If a, b, c are in AP, then $\\overline{b} \\geq \\frac{\\overline{a} + \\overline{c}}{2}$ and b is called the arithmetic mean of a and c."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.436,
            0.967,
            0.562,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
